{"abstract":"When mass protests and violent crackdown followed the 2009 Iranian presidential election, Western mass media found themselves in a precarious situation: eager to report on the unfolding events, but without access to them; save through snapshots and text bites posted to content- sharing sites by unknown users. Basing news coverage on such content challenged journalistic understandings of credibility as produced by professional routines, thus disturbing the foundation of epistemic authority on which professional journalism builds. Neglecting it, however, would challenge journalism’s ability to portray anything at all. This article investigates how the positioning of citizen micro-journalism was textually negotiated in news reports by attributing different degrees of epistemic authority to citizen-made content. It argues that the strategies used greatly privilege the unknown image vis-a`-vis the unknown verbal report. While verbal reports are treated as illustrative of communication practices or attributed doubt, images are allowed to represent the crisis, and frequently made indistinguishable from professionally produced content. Furthermore, in attributing citizen-made content to news agencies and mediation channels, the incorporation practices treat intermediation as a source of credibility. Deconstructing the process of constructing epistemologically authoritative news thus highlights how mediation, news values, source practices, and image conventions are relied on to perform credibility. KEYWORDS content-sharing sites; epistemic authority; images; Iran; journalism; remediation; social media","author_count":1,"contributors":["Andersen, Rune Saugmann"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-542921"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"remediating iranelection journalistic strategies for positioning citizen made snapshots and text bites from the 000000002009 iranian post election conflict","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/17512786.2012.663593","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-542921","identifier":[{"id":"85011026759","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-fa748e8f-546a-4aaf-9a71-f4e2df93cf59"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-877B23CEA56B5607E3F2A43D87D6E48D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/17512786.2012.663593","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/17512786.2012.663593","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Department of Political Science, Department of Political Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Rune Saugmann","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"d0034342-7947-4ea5-8fe8-f91c8dfdc87d","name":"Andersen, Rune Saugmann","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1080/17512786.2012.663593","issn":["14616211","17512786"],"issn_org":["17512786"],"issue":"3","journal":"Journalism Practice","pages":"317-336","status":"p","title":"Journalism Practice","volume":"6"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85011026759"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-fa748e8f-546a-4aaf-9a71-f4e2df93cf59"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"REMEDIATING #IRANELECTION : Journalistic strategies for positioning citizen-made snapshots and text bites from the 2009 Iranian post-election conflict","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-542921","year":"2012","yearsub":"2012"}
{"abstract":"The 2009 post-election crisis in Iran positioned Iranian unrest on front pages, newscasts, and blogs around the world, spurring intense debate and many attempts to influence the power struggle between Iranian authorities and the ‘green’ dissident movement. This paper explores how international news media converged on a narrative representing the green movement in terms of their use of popular online media, linking western news audiences to the green movement in a story of ‘common identity through common online culture’. This news narrative stresses protesters’ online media practices, especially their use of popular microblogging service Twitter, constructing a sense of proximity between western mass media audiences and the ‘green movement’. It also constitutes the conflict as international through its virtuality, spurring intervention by discursively downplaying the ‘internal affairs’ barrier against intervention, creating an issue-specific vacuum for foreign interventions on behalf of the opposition. It is argued that radically different actors sharing a perceived stake in the political properties of cyberspace used this vacuum in discursive struggle that enlarged the Iran crisis to mirror a battle for the properties and values of cyberspace, struggling over the meaning of free online speech and primacy of states and sovereignty in international relations.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Andersen, Rune Saugmann"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-447165"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"iran election cyber statehood and the mass mediated fight for cyberspace","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dco","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-447165","ids":["ku-59da2873-fd95-40cc-9a4f-2a70ac1215b7"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-2C4454984C11A280C814CEC77C2DCFA2"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Department of Political Science, Department of Political Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Rune Saugmann","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"d0034342-7947-4ea5-8fe8-f91c8dfdc87d","name":"Andersen, Rune Saugmann","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-59da2873-fd95-40cc-9a4f-2a70ac1215b7"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Iran Election, Cyber-Statehood, and the Mass Mediated Fight for Cyberspace","type":"Conference Poster","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-447165","year":"2011","yearsub":"2011"}
